<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Bavand dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">651 CE – 1349 CE (approx.) · capital Sari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the longest-lived Iranian dynasties, ruling parts of Tabaristan on the Caspian for almost seven centuries from the Arab conquest to the Mongol age.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition traces the Bavandids to Bav, a descendant of the Sasanian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawus-son-of-kavad-i">Kawus</a>, who established himself in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> around 651. The family survived the Abbasid conquest of the lowlands in 760 by holding the highland region of Firim.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty ruled in three main branches: the Kayusiyya (to 1074), the Ispahbadiyya (1074 to 1210), centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sari-iran">Sari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a>, and the Kinkhwariyya (1238 to 1349). Its rulers kept the ancient title ispahbad and long remained Zoroastrian, converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the 9th century and later favouring Shia Islam. Sharvin I fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a>. They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in turn of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, while patronising Persian letters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1349 the last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-dawla-hasan">Fakhr al-Dawla Hasan</a>, was murdered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiya-afrasiyab">Afrasiyab Chelavi</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/afrasiyabids-of-mazandaran">Afrasiyabids of Mazandaran</a>, ending the line.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bavand-dynasty.jpg" alt="Bavand dynasty" width="320" height="244"><figcaption>Image: Swordrist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bavandids_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>651 CE – 1349 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sharvin I, Ardashir I, Fakhr al-Dawla Hasan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zoroastrianism, later Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tabari, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bavandids, Ispahbads of Tabaristan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=bavand-dynasty" aria-label="Bavand dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6013 859 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bavand dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6333 1115L6134 1224M6333 1115L6481 1127M6333 1115L6316 1118"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6481 1127h0M6316 1118h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6333 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6333 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sari. <a href="/atlas?year=1000&amp;focus=bavand-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
